PLACES=[
# ---- Barkhamsted: the two state forests on the West Branch ----
N('peoples-forest-nature-museum','Peoples State Forest Nature Museum','Barkhamsted','ct','civic','standing',41.92528,-72.99722,'site','public','drive','','1934–35',
  'A rubblestone museum built in 1934–35 by a Civilian Conservation Corps crew from Camp White, across the river, and dedicated by Governor Wilbur Cross in 1935. When it first closed is given two ways: in 1942, when the CCC was disbanded (the museum\'s own history), or in the early 1950s (Wikipedia). It stood unused for decades and reopened in 1992, first as "The Stone Museum"; it took back its old name in 2007.',
  'A one-storey fieldstone building with a bell-cast shingled roof and a fieldstone chimney and fireplace. Inside: a bluestone floor, walls panelled in American chestnut cut after the blight killed the trees, and exposed queen-post roof trusses with wrought-iron straps. The displays are mounted Connecticut animals and birds, forest and local history, and a diorama of Camp White made by a former CCC man.',
  'The CCC built a museum, then walked away from it for fifty years.',
  'On Greenwoods Road in Peoples State Forest (CT DEEP). The forest is open daily, 8 a.m. to sunset, per the DEEP park page. The museum is open Memorial Day through Labor Day weekend, admission free, per the museum\'s site (days of the week not given there; Wikipedia says Friday to Monday). Phone (860) 379-2469. Mapped lots on Greenwoods Road and East River Road.',
  [W('Peoples State Forest Nature Museum'),L('Peoples State Forest Nature Museum','https://psfnaturemuseum.org'),PSF],conf='mixed',
  th=[41.92288,-72.9954],thn='Lot on Greenwoods Road, Peoples State Forest',brick='no'),
N('american-legion-ccc-shelter','American Legion Forest CCC shelter and Camp White','Barkhamsted','ct','civic','standing',41.9404,-73.0148,'site','public','walk','','1933–1941',
  'The Civilian Conservation Corps ran Camp White in American Legion State Forest from 1933 (DEEP\'s forestry page says 1934) to 1941; the crews built roads, firebreaks, trails and four log shelters. This three-sided log shelter is the only one of the four left, and one of two CCC shelters to survive in Connecticut. It stands on loop three of the youth group camping area, which is the old camp site.',
  'A low three-sided log shelter on stone piers, with a roof short in front and long at the back (now of modern material, first wood shingles). DEEP says remnants of Camp White can still be seen in and around the youth group camping area, and that the stone steps on the Henry Buck Trail were laid by the CCC.',
  'The last of the CCC\'s four log shelters on the West Branch.',
  'American Legion State Forest (CT DEEP), off West River Road; open daily 8 a.m. to sunset per the DEEP park page. The youth group camping area is reserved for qualified youth groups: when it is booked, look from West River Road and do not walk through a group\'s camp.',
  [W('American Legion Forest CCC Shelter'),ALSF,LBOX],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('cheese-box-factory-henry-buck','Cheese box factory ruin, Henry Buck Trail','Barkhamsted','ct','industry','ruins',41.93623,-73.01297,'area','public','hike','','early 1800s',
  'A small water-powered factory that made wooden boxes for shipping local cheese in the early 1800s, high on a brook above the West Branch. A little earthen dam upstream held the water, which came down a wooden sluice to a wheel pit under the building and turned a paddle geared to the machines above.',
  'About three-quarters of a mile up the Henry Buck Trail from its southern trailhead, where the trail levels out: what looks like a cellar hole with a big stone wall behind it is the wheel pit and the factory\'s foundation, with a stepped water channel into the pit. Just upstream the trail crosses the washed-out earthen dam. DEEP asks visitors not to climb into the pit or on the wall, which are caving in. A charcoal mound is further along the trail.',
  'A cheese-box factory whose cellar hole is really a wheel pit.',
  'American Legion State Forest (CT DEEP); open daily 8 a.m. to sunset per the DEEP park page. The Henry Buck Trail is a 2.1-mile loop with two trailheads on West River Road about 1,500 feet apart; the southern one is a few hundred feet beyond the second entrance to Legion Road, with parking along the road or on Legion Road (DEEP letterbox page). Steep, rocky and slippery when wet. The pin is a point on the trail from OpenStreetMap, not the ruin.',
  [LBOX,ALSF],th=[41.93886,-73.00904],thn='Lot on Legion Road, American Legion State Forest',brick='no'),
N('saville-dam','Saville Dam','Barkhamsted','ct','water','standing',41.91,-72.95472,'site','view','drive','drowned','1936–1940',
  'The Metropolitan District Commission began buying land here in 1927 and building the dam in 1936, while the valley to the north was stripped of timber and buildings. First called the Bill\'s Brook Dam, it was finished in May 1940 and named for the MDC\'s chief engineer, Caleb Mills Saville; the reservoir behind it did not fill until 1948. It is Hartford\'s main water supply, and it drowned the village of Barkhamsted Hollow and many farms.',
  'An earthen dam with masonry work, 135 feet high and 1,950 feet long, with an open spillway at its west end. Route 318 runs along the top. What else survives of the masonry (a gatehouse, parapets) was not checked.',
  'A dam that drowned a village to give Hartford its water.',
  'Owned by the Metropolitan District Commission. Connecticut Route 318 (Saville Dam Road) crosses the top, so it can be seen from the road. A lot is mapped on Saville Dam Road west of the dam; who owns it and the MDC\'s rules for walking there were not checked (the MDC rules page would not open). Reservoir land is watershed: keep to the road.',
  [W('Saville Dam')],th=[41.90822,-72.95917],thn='Mapped lot on Saville Dam Road (owner not checked)',brick='?'),
# ---- Riverton ----
N('union-church-riverton','Union Church (St. Paul\'s), Riverton','Barkhamsted','ct','worship','standing',41.96229,-73.02011,'exact','view','drive','','1829',
  'Built in 1829 as a meeting house shared by several denominations. As each built its own church this one emptied, and in 1880 it became St. Paul\'s Episcopal Church, which closed in 1971. The revived Hitchcock Chair Company bought it and opened a museum, which closed in the early 2000s along with the chair factory; since then it has been a glass-blower\'s studio.',
  'A granite church of randomly coursed ashlar with Greek Revival proportions and tall Gothic Revival windows; a square tower in front has a stone lower stage and wooden upper stages with corner pinnacles. Wikipedia says the interior keeps many original features. Whether and when the studio lets visitors in was not checked.',
  'A granite meeting house turned chair museum turned glass studio.',
  'At 3 Robertsville Road, Riverton, now Peter Greenwood\'s glass-blowing studio and gallery (private business). Look from the road; the studio\'s hours were not checked. A lot on Riverton Road just south is marked paid parking during events.',
  [W('Union Church/St. Paul\'s Church'),W('Riverton Historic District (Barkhamsted, Connecticut)')],brick='no'),
N('old-riverton-inn','Old Riverton Inn','Barkhamsted','ct','hotel','standing',41.96278,-73.01694,'site','view','drive','hotels','c.1811',
  'An inn built about 1811, probably by Jesse Ives, facing the east end of the 1790 bridge over the East Branch Farmington River, the only crossing for miles, where the road from New Hartford to Massachusetts met the road to Winsted. Wikipedia says it has taken in travellers ever since; it was listed on the National Register in 1992.',
  'A two-and-a-half-storey wooden inn on a brick basement that shows on the street side, where the slope makes it look three storeys tall. Road widening took its 19th-century porch; the old doorway surround was moved to the south side, and a broken pediment marks where the front door was. Interior not checked.',
  'Two hundred years of travellers at the only bridge for miles.',
  'At 436 East River Road, Riverton; a private inn. Look from the road, or go in as a guest; whether it is still open as an inn today was not checked.',
  [W('Old Riverton Inn')],brick='some'),
# ---- Winsted ----
N('gilbert-clock-factory','Gilbert Clock Factory','Winchester','ct','industry','standing',41.93028,-73.05806,'site','view','drive','','1870–1964',
  'William L. Gilbert moved to Winchester in 1840 to make cheap wall and shelf clocks for the mass market, and built this case shop on the Still River in 1870–71. The works on Wallens Street grew to four large buildings and more than 500 workers, and ran until 1964. The buildings south of Wallens Street burned in 1975. Radium was used here to paint luminous clock hands; the federal nuclear regulators surveyed the buildings in 2018, found a few spots with raised readings in one building and no public health risk, and closed the matter.',
  'Two four-storey Italianate brick factory buildings north of Wallens Street, one built right on the river bank and one up the hill, now apartments. Interior not open: private housing.',
  'One of Connecticut\'s big cheap-clock works, now flats.',
  'At 13 Wallens Street, Winsted; private apartments. Look from Wallens Street.',
  [W('Gilbert Clock Factory')],brick='yes'),
N('winsted-hosiery-mill','Winsted Hosiery Mill','Winchester','ct','industry','standing',41.92333,-73.05889,'site','view','drive','','1882–c.1910',
  'William L. Gilbert founded the Winsted Hosiery Company in 1882 to make men\'s hosiery and underwear of cotton and wool. Its first mill burned in 1900, and over the next ten years the company built the complex that stands now. It grew until about 1935 and lost workers during and after the Second World War. Little altered, it is now split among several industrial and commercial tenants.',
  'Six buildings either side of Whiting Street along the Still River: five of brick, and a stone office building at the corner of Holabird Avenue and Whiting Street with a long, narrow brick wing running south.',
  'A whole hosiery mill of 1900–1910, barely changed.',
  'At 210 Holabird Avenue, Winsted; private businesses. Look from Holabird Avenue and Whiting Street.',
  [W('Winsted Hosiery Mill')],brick='yes'),
N('winchester-soldiers-monument','Winchester Soldiers\' Monument','Winchester','ct','landmark','standing',41.92458,-73.07285,'exact','public','drive','','1889–1890',
  'A Civil War memorial paid for by the town\'s Grand Army of the Republic post, designed by Robert Hill of Waterbury and finished in 1890, with a bronze soldier by George Bissell. It is one of the most elaborate Civil War memorials in Connecticut. An iron staircase was planned inside but never fitted after a dispute with the contractor, and a wooden stair meant to be temporary went in instead.',
  'A three-stage tower of local granite ashlar on a round grassy hilltop, its lower walls sloping in, with battlements and a round corner turret topped by the bronze soldier holding the colours. A battlemented arch leads to a path up the hill; a fountain of 1977–78 stands near the tower. Whether the tower is ever opened was not checked.',
  'A castle on a hill for Winsted\'s Civil War soldiers.',
  'In the park at the south end of Crown Street, Winsted, with a parking area on Crown Street behind a low stone wall. Who runs the park and its hours were not checked; the federal public-land map does not show it.',
  [W('Winchester Soldiers\' Monument')],brick='no'),
N('solomon-rockwell-house','Solomon Rockwell House','Winchester','ct','civic','standing',41.92414,-73.0783,'exact','public','drive','','mid-1700s–1813',
  'Built in 1813 for Solomon Rockwell, who owned two iron forges in town, to a design by William Swift, the carpenter who built the Colebrook Store. An older house of the mid-18th century survives as one of its ells. The Winchester Historical Society has owned it since the 1920s and runs it as a museum.',
  'A fine Greek Revival house with a two-storey columned temple front, a string of ells and a small carriage barn. Inside: 19th-century things from Winsted\'s factories and the Civil War, and the town\'s fire museum.',
  'An ironmaster\'s temple-front house, now the town\'s attic.',
  'At 225 Prospect Street (Wikipedia gives 226), at Lake Street, Winsted; Winchester Historical Society. Open by appointment (Wikipedia); a Lakeville Journal article (its date not checked) gave Sundays 1 to 4 p.m. through October, or by appointment. The society\'s own page was not found, so current hours were not checked.',
  [W('Solomon Rockwell House'),L('Lakeville Journal: Historical Society rich with artifacts','https://lakevillejournal.com/historical-society-rich-artifacts-days-gone')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
# ---- Colebrook and Norfolk ----
N('colebrook-store','Colebrook Store','Colebrook','ct','town','standing',41.98944,-73.09667,'exact','view','drive','','1812',
  'Built in 1812 by William Swift, a local carpenter who mostly made bedsteads, and run as the village general store ever since (Wikipedia). It stands in Colebrook Center, a village that has changed little since about 1860.',
  'A wooden store with a two-storey Federal temple front: a full pediment with a half-round fan, on four slender Tuscan columns, and a flush-boarded front behind them with a second-floor loading door. Inside, from the late 1800s: hardwood floors, tin ceilings and counters.',
  'Two hundred years of a general store behind a temple front.',
  'At 559 Colebrook Road (Route 183) at Rockwell Road, Colebrook Center. A store: go in when it is open. Its hours, and whether it trades today, were not checked.',
  [W('Colebrook Store'),W('Colebrook Center Historic District')],brick='no'),
N('haystack-mountain-tower','Haystack Mountain Tower','Norfolk','ct','landmark','standing',42.00473,-73.20839,'exact','public','hike','','1929',
  'A stone observation tower given in 1929, with the land for the state park, by Ellen Battell Stoeckel in memory of her husband Carl; it cost about $50,000. It was designed by Ehrick K. Rossiter in a medieval Tudor Revival manner, and once carried a beacon light, now gone.',
  'A 34-foot round tower of dark grey granite quarried on the spot, laid at random with deep-set mortar, with a stone string course outside. Concrete steps and then a metal stair climb to a platform under a conical roof with eight openings; on a clear day you see three states and, to the south, Long Island Sound.',
  'A Gilded Age widow\'s granite tower with three states in view.',
  'Haystack Mountain State Park (CT DEEP), on Route 272 just north of Norfolk village. Open daily 8 a.m. to sunset. From late April to November the park road is open to cars up to a small lot below the summit; from November to late April the road is closed and it is walk-in only. Free; dogs on leash. From the lot it is a short climb; the rocky Tower Loop Trail, about a mile, starts near the entrance (DEEP page).',
  [W('Haystack Mountain Tower'),L('CT DEEP: Haystack Mountain State Park','https://portal.ct.gov/deep/state-parks/parks/haystack-mountain-state-park')],brick='no'),
N('dennis-hill-tamarack-lodge','Tamarack Lodge, Dennis Hill','Norfolk','ct','landmark','standing',41.94896,-73.19894,'exact','public','drive','','1908',
  'Frederic Shepard Dennis, a New York surgeon who summered in Norfolk, called his estate Tamarac and in 1908 had Alfredo S. G. Taylor design a summer lodge on the summit. Andrew Carnegie, President Taft and Walter Damrosch were his guests there. He gave the estate to the state in 1935; DEEP says the summit building later served as a lookout in the Second World War.',
  'A stone bungalow with a standing-seam metal roof on the open summit, now an open picnic pavilion: the windows are out and most original fittings gone, with lofts round the main room that were probably sleeping space. Small pavilions beside it, and wide views to Haystack Mountain, Mount Greylock and the Green Mountains.',
  'A surgeon\'s summit lodge where Carnegie and Taft came to dine.',
  'Dennis Hill State Park (CT DEEP), 519 Litchfield Road (Route 272), Norfolk. Open daily 8 a.m. to sunset; the road up is open late April to November, and the rest of the year it is walk-in only. No buses of 30 or more seats (DEEP page).',
  [W('Dennis Hill State Park'),L('CT DEEP: Dennis Hill State Park','https://portal.ct.gov/deep/state-parks/parks/dennis-hill-state-park')],brick='no'),
N('norfolk-downs-shelter','Norfolk Downs golf shelter','Norfolk','ct','amuse','standing',41.98111,-73.21611,'site','view','drive','','1907',
  'The Eldridge sisters opened Norfolk Downs, a public nine-hole golf course, in 1897, and in 1907 had Alfredo S. G. Taylor design this pavilion for it. They would not allow golf on Sundays, so other townspeople started the private Norfolk Country Club next door; the two courses never merged, and the Downs was abandoned. The shelter is seen as a forerunner of Taylor\'s lodge on Dennis Hill.',
  'A one-storey rubblestone shelter, an octagon under a low roof with open terraces either side and a stone chimney; the glass and fittings are gone, leaving the rough rafters to be seen. It stands in woods and clearings south of the Country Club.',
  'The clubhouse of a golf course that would not play on Sundays.',
  'On Golf Drive, Norfolk. Who owns the shelter and its land was not checked: look from Golf Drive and do not cross the land without asking.',
  [W('Norfolk Downs Shelter')],brick='no'),
# ---- New Hartford and Collinsville ----
N('gillettes-grist-mill','Gillette\'s Grist Mill','New Hartford','ct','industry','standing',41.83833,-73.02472,'site','view','drive','','c.1840s–1880',
  'Joseph Gillette ran sawmills, a gristmill, a carding mill and a turning shop on the Nepaug River in the early 1800s. His children took over the gristmill in 1840, but grain milling was moving to big regional mills and this one shut by 1880. When the building went up is not known; it looks mid-19th-century, and the machinery dates from about 1870.',
  'A wooden mill built like a barn on a high stone foundation with a big wheel pit, on the north bank of the Nepaug just west of Maple Hollow Road. A rare survivor with its water wheel and the iron gears and shafts that drove the stones, and traces of the races either side. Interior not open; whether the wheel can be seen from the road was not checked.',
  'A Connecticut gristmill that still has its wheel.',
  'Maple Hollow Road, New Hartford, among houses: private property. Look from the road only.',
  [W('Gillette\'s Grist Mill')],brick='no'),
N('collins-company-collinsville','Collins Company axe works and Canton Historical Museum','Canton','ct','industry','standing',41.81044,-72.92388,'site','view','walk','','1826–1966',
  'Samuel W. and David C. Collins and their cousin William Wells bought an old gristmill on the Farmington River in 1826 and started making axes, at first eight men making eight a day. They were the first ready-to-use axes made in the United States. The company built the workers\' houses, a church and a bank, and the village took its name; it sold machetes in 35 countries, built its own dams for power around 1870, and made 1,300 kinds of edge tools. After the 1955 flood took the railroad it lost ground to foreign makers, and it closed in 1966.',
  'The old factory buildings ramble along the river among the mill races and are rented to small businesses; some were pulled down and some stand empty. The Canton Historical Museum is in an 1865 company building on Front Street, with three floors on Canton and the Collins Company (a Collins fire engine among them) and a research library upstairs.',
  'The axe works that named its town and went to the gold rush.',
  'The factory buildings are private business premises: look from Main Street, Front Street and the bridge. The Canton Historical Museum (11 Front Street, 860-693-2793) gives holiday hours of Saturday and Sunday 10 to 5, Tuesday, Wednesday and Friday noon to 4, and Thursday noon to 7 for November 15 to December 6; hours at other times of year were not checked. Admission by donation. Mapped parking on Front Street by the museum.',
  [W('Collinsville, Connecticut'),L('ConnecticutHistory.org: World-renowned Maker of Axes, the Collins Company','https://connecticuthistory.org/?p=709'),L('Canton Historical Museum','https://cantonmuseum.org/locations/chm/'),L('Buildings of New England: Collins Axe Company Factory','https://buildingsofnewengland.com/2021/11/30/collins-axe-company-factory-1826-1966/')],
  th=[41.80996,-72.92323],thn='Lot on Front Street by the Canton Historical Museum',brick='?'),
]

